¶
'''tread-fowl†''' (tred′foul), ''n.'' [ME. ''tredefowl''; < ''tread'', ''v.'', + obj. ''fowl''.] A cock.

{{smaller block|Thow woldest han been a ''tredefowel'' aright.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Monk's Tale, l. 57.}}}}

'''treading''' (tred′ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''tread'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of setting down the foot; a step.

{{smaller block|My feet were almost gone, my ''treadings'' had well-nigh slipt.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Psalter, Ps. lxxiii. 2.}}
''Treading'' consists in pressing and kneading the clay-paste little by little with bare feet.
{{right|''Glass-making'', p. 30.}}}}

'''2.''' That which is trampled down.

{{smaller block|The off horse walks on the grass, but outside of the line of cut; consequently, his ''treadings'' are met by the machine on the return journey, and cut clean.
{{right|''Ure'', Dict., IV. 28.}}}}

